<section class="page-hero wrap"><p class="eyebrow">The technology</p><h1>Built in pieces.<br><em>Connected by purpose.</em></h1><p class="page-intro">An adaptable clinical core, reusable open-source components, and a care experience designed around people.</p></section>

<section class="section foundation-section"><div class="wrap foundation-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">The open-source core</p><h2>Meet HiperHealth.</h2><p>HiperHealth is the computational library at the heart of the plan. Its public Python library provides composable clinical workflow stages and a plugin system for installable “skills.”</p><p>Rather than one fixed pipeline, the architecture lets developers compose and extend the workflow. The ambition is to bring in the specialty capabilities needed for a particular care setting.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button-light" href="{{ site.library }}">Explore the source <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="{{ site.libraryDocs }}">Read the documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div><div class="code-portrait"><span class="graphic-label">Composable by design</span><div class="code-line">Screening <span>01</span></div><div class="code-line">Intake <span>02</span></div><div class="code-line">Diagnosis <span>03</span></div><div class="code-line">Examinations <span>04</span></div><div class="code-line">Treatment <span>05</span></div><div class="code-line">Prescription <span>06</span></div><p>Skills can extend one or more stages.<br>Clinical deployment requires evaluation.</p></div></div></section>

<section class="section wrap"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Where we are</p><h2>An existing foundation.<br>A clear development agenda.</h2><p>The public library and the planned Hipercare service are at different stages. Here is the distinction.</p></div><div class="status-columns"><article><p class="pill">Documented in the library</p><h3>A core to build on</h3><ul class="feature-list"><li>Composable pipeline stages and installable skill plugins.</li><li>Persistent sessions that can resume as information arrives.</li><li>Checks for information required by a skill.</li><li>Built-in capabilities for diagnostic suggestions, report extraction, and de-identification.</li></ul><p class="small-note">Software functionality is not evidence of clinical effectiveness. See the <a href="{{ site.library }}/blob/main/README.md">library’s current documentation</a> for scope and limitations.</p></article><article><p class="pill pill-outline">Planned for Hipercare</p><h3>A connected care experience</h3><ul class="feature-list"><li>Longitudinal patient records connected to the care pipeline.</li><li>Medication interaction checks to support clinician review.</li><li>Pathways that account for protocols, coverage, and patient circumstances.</li><li>Future-risk insights to support prevention, subject to research and validation.</li></ul><p class="small-note">These are development intentions, not a list of live or clinically validated services.</p></article></div></section>

<section class="section soft-section"><div class="wrap split-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">Our open-source philosophy</p><h2>Share the foundations.<br>Build with intention.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Hipercare is planned as a proprietary service. Its philosophy is to break down the technology into small, useful open-source projects, then orchestrate them into a coherent experience.</p><p>That distinction matters: an open component can be studied and improved independently, while the complete service has its own product responsibilities.</p><p>HiperHealth is one of those foundations. We welcome contributors who care about clear interfaces, reproducible workflows, documentation, and useful building blocks.</p><a class="text-link" href="{{ site.library }}">Contribute to HiperHealth <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section>

<section class="section wrap"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Development priorities</p><h2>Trust has to be<br>part of the architecture.</h2></div><div class="principles"><article><span class="principle-number">01</span><h3>Clinical oversight</h3><p>Make recommendations reviewable, preserve uncertainty, and keep care decisions with qualified professionals.</p></article><article><span class="principle-number">02</span><h3>Responsible records</h3><p>Develop patient-record handling around appropriate access controls, consent, traceability, and data protection.</p></article><article><span class="principle-number">03</span><h3>Evaluation in context</h3><p>Assess components and workflows for the populations, specialties, and settings where they may be used.</p></article></div></section>
